#### 2.2.3 Barrel Shower Maximum Detector: Bsmd

The STAR BSMD provides finer spatial resolution and shower profile measurement compared with the BEMC, which has much larger segmentation [1]. Each of the 4800 towers of the BEMC spans \(\Delta\phi\times\Delta\eta=0.05(radian)\times 0.05\) which corresponds to a tower size larger than \(10cm\times 10cm\) at the radius of the inner surface of the detector at \(\eta=0\) and increasing toward \(\eta=1\). While the BEMC towers provide precise energy measurements for isolated electromagnetic showers, the high spatial resolution and shower profile information provided by the BSMD is essential for \(\pi^{0}\) reconstruction, direct \(\gamma\) identification, and electron identification. Figure 2.10 shows the conceptual design of the STAR BSMD. It is located at about 5 radiation lengths depth from the inner surface of the BEMC modules. Figure 2.5 shows that the shower usually develops to its maximum at a depth around 5 radiation lengths.

The STAR BSMD has a double layer design. A two sided aluminum extrusion provides ground channels for two independent planes of gold plated tungsten wires. Each of these wires spans \(\Delta\eta=1\) on each side of the extrusion. There are two independent PC board cathode planes with strips etched in the \(\eta\) and \(\phi\) directions respectively to sense the induced charge from the charge amplification near the wires. These perpendicular strips allow reconstruction of a two dimensional image of the shower. One set of strips are called the \(\eta\) strips. These \(\eta\) strips are perpendicular to the wires and provide an image of the shower spatial distribution in the \(\eta\) direction. Each of these \(\eta\) strips spans \(\Delta\phi\approx 0.1(radian)\), which corresponds to the module width of about \(23cm\). There are two groups of \(\eta\) strips. One group is narrower and each narrower strip spans about \(1.537cm\) in Z. There are 75 of these narrower strips spanning from 0 to 0.5 in \(\eta\). The other group is wider and each wider strip spans about \(1.961cm\) in Z. There areFigure 2.10: Schematic illustration of the double layer STAR BSMD. Two independent wire layers separated by an aluminum extrusion image electromagnetic showers in the \(\eta\) and \(\phi\) directions on corresponding pad layers.

also 75 of these wider strips spanning from 0.5 to 1 in \(\eta\). The other set of strips are called the \(\phi\) strips. \(\phi\) strips are parallel to the wires. These strips are about \(1.33cm\) in width and 0.1 units in \(\eta\). 15 of these strips form a patch/sub-division, which spans \(\Delta\phi\approx 0.1\) and \(\Delta\eta=0.1\). There are 10 patches in each module. The BSMD is essentially a multi-wire proportional chamber, in which the strips read out signals using gas amplification. Some important features of the double sided BSMD design include improved reliability, improved functionality in high occupancy environment, improved hadron rejection and \(\gamma/\pi^{0}\) separation and simplified mechanical construction. Figure 2.11 shows the schematics of the wires and strips in a STAR BSMD module.

The STAR BSMD design parameters and specifications are summarized in table 2.3.

